1
00:00:02,195 --> 00:00:03,489
[MYCROFT]
Regarding the Jack the Ripper

2
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:05,324
incident from the other day.

3
00:00:05,365 --> 00:00:06,826
[MYCROFT]
A common enemy appeared before

4
00:00:06,867 --> 00:00:10,663
the Yard and the public
moments before chaos ensued.

5
00:00:10,704 --> 00:00:14,834
Within the blink of an eye,
hostility turned to solidarity.

6
00:00:16,209 --> 00:00:18,253
One might say it
offered a brief glimpse

7
00:00:18,295 --> 00:00:21,089
into the Moriarty Plan,
frankly speaking.

8
00:00:21,131 --> 00:00:25,803
[ALBERT grunts]
Nonetheless, this incident...

9
00:00:25,845 --> 00:00:29,765
[MYCROFT]
Indeed, it is not over yet.

10
00:00:29,807 --> 00:00:33,560
Despite the fact "Jack the
Ripper" is no longer among us,

11
00:00:33,602 --> 00:00:36,522
the Yard has still
arrested a suspect.

12
00:00:38,732 --> 00:00:40,400
[LOUIS] Obviously,
they've falsely accused

13
00:00:40,442 --> 00:00:43,236
this man based on
fabricated evidence.

14
00:00:43,278 --> 00:00:46,114
[WILLIAM] Yes, after
allowing Jack to escape,

15
00:00:46,156 --> 00:00:47,825
the Yard is disgraced.

16
00:00:47,867 --> 00:00:51,244
Of course, they would resort
to such extreme measures.

17
00:00:51,286 --> 00:00:54,247
No surprise.
So, what's the plan?

18
00:00:54,289 --> 00:00:57,001
First, we set this
innocent man free.

19
00:00:57,042 --> 00:00:59,837
Then find the person
that accused him

20
00:00:59,879 --> 00:01:03,674
and see that they
receive their just rewards.

21
00:01:03,716 --> 00:01:07,260
[ALBERT] In short, following
your brother's decision,

22
00:01:07,302 --> 00:01:09,346
we will allow the
case to go unsolved and

23
00:01:09,388 --> 00:01:12,474
remain just that--a mystery.

24
00:01:13,893 --> 00:01:15,895
[MYCROFT]
I wish for your success, Albert.

25
00:01:15,936 --> 00:01:18,438
Best of luck.

26
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:22,776
You're doing rather fine work,
you and your two brothers.

27
00:01:22,818 --> 00:01:26,363
How kind, I hope the
same for you and yours.

28
00:01:26,405 --> 00:01:31,117
Yes, that depends
entirely on him.

29
00:01:32,077 --> 00:01:33,871
[HOLMES grunts]

30
00:03:04,795 --> 00:03:05,879
[CITIZEN 17A]
Send out Jack!

31
00:03:05,921 --> 00:03:07,965
[CITIZEN 17B]
Hang the scoundrel by the neck!

32
00:03:08,007 --> 00:03:10,509
[CITIZEN 17C] What's the wait?
Bring him to justice!

33
00:03:11,551 --> 00:03:13,470
[PATERSON]
Hell of a commotion out there.

34
00:03:13,512 --> 00:03:17,975
[gasps] Paterson.

35
00:03:18,017 --> 00:03:22,146
It's looking an awful lot like
Bloody Sunday, don't you think?

36
00:03:22,187 --> 00:03:27,275
That town doctor we arrested,
the man has a clean record.

37
00:03:27,317 --> 00:03:29,402
The officers were
following direct orders

38
00:03:29,444 --> 00:03:31,113
from Chief Inspector Arterton.

39
00:03:31,155 --> 00:03:34,366
Apparently, they found evidence
he was involved in the murders.

40
00:03:35,534 --> 00:03:37,327
What do you think?

41
00:03:37,369 --> 00:03:40,413
[PATERSON] Hm. The fact that
you're asking me that question

42
00:03:40,455 --> 00:03:42,541
means you have
doubts about the arrest;

43
00:03:42,582 --> 00:03:44,376
that we've the wrong man.

44
00:03:44,417 --> 00:03:46,419
[LESTRADE grunts]

45
00:03:46,461 --> 00:03:49,297
[AMES] Oh! Good day,
Chief Inspector Arterton.

46
00:03:49,339 --> 00:03:52,009
[ARTERTON] Not until you
silence the rabble outside!

47
00:03:52,051 --> 00:03:53,385
All of them, sir?

48
00:03:53,426 --> 00:03:55,721
Just drag two or three of
their leaders out of the crowd!

49
00:03:55,762 --> 00:03:56,638
Duff 'em up proper,

50
00:03:56,680 --> 00:03:58,515
and toss 'em back out
as an example!

51
00:03:58,557 --> 00:04:01,185
[AMES]
We can't do that, sir.

52
00:04:02,519 --> 00:04:06,065
[LESTRADE] Our Jack the Ripper
is likely falsely accused.

53
00:04:06,106 --> 00:04:11,403
And if I had to guess,
Arterton's behind it.

54
00:04:11,444 --> 00:04:14,114
And do you have any proof?

55
00:04:14,156 --> 00:04:15,448
I mean, let's be frank.

56
00:04:15,490 --> 00:04:17,743
We are talking
about our boss here.

57
00:04:17,784 --> 00:04:19,703
The CID's top man.

58
00:04:19,745 --> 00:04:21,371
[LESTRADE grunts]

59
00:04:30,089 --> 00:04:32,049
Arterton has a history
of using what some

60
00:04:32,091 --> 00:04:35,719
might call heavy-handed tactics
in order to produce results.

61
00:04:35,761 --> 00:04:38,889
There's a reason he got
promoted to where he is today.

62
00:04:42,059 --> 00:04:43,518
And considering
how Jack the Ripper

63
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:45,353
escaped right from
under his nose,

64
00:04:45,395 --> 00:04:48,607
it was only a matter of time
before heads began to roll.

65
00:04:48,648 --> 00:04:51,860
Ample motivation to falsify
evidence if you were he.

66
00:04:51,902 --> 00:04:54,780
[gasps] And we're going
to let him go unchallenged

67
00:04:54,821 --> 00:04:56,615
while he convicts
an innocent man?

68
00:04:56,656 --> 00:04:59,409
Scotland Yard is supposed
to uphold the law!

69
00:04:59,451 --> 00:05:01,161
Kindly lower your voice.

70
00:05:01,203 --> 00:05:03,205
[LESTRADE]
You're-- You're right.

71
00:05:03,247 --> 00:05:06,750
So, how are you going to
prove the charges are false?

72
00:05:06,792 --> 00:05:09,461
Working on it.

73
00:05:09,502 --> 00:05:11,171
Well, you had
better hurry yourself,

74
00:05:11,213 --> 00:05:13,215
because he will
likely confess to the crimes

75
00:05:13,257 --> 00:05:15,467
following Arterton's
script to the letter.

76
00:05:15,508 --> 00:05:17,052
[LESTRADE grunts]

77
00:05:19,471 --> 00:05:21,389
[PATERSON] I don't know
if you're aware of this,

78
00:05:21,431 --> 00:05:23,725
but Arterton keeps
a secret ledger.

79
00:05:23,767 --> 00:05:25,435
[LESTRADE]
A ledger?

80
00:05:25,477 --> 00:05:27,479
Of course! For a lie this size,

81
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:30,649
there has to be a lot of
off-the-books money at play.

82
00:05:30,690 --> 00:05:33,986
If we could track down any money
diverted from the investigation,

83
00:05:34,027 --> 00:05:37,239
that'd be all the
evidence we need.

84
00:05:37,281 --> 00:05:40,659
Truly, the definitive
proof required.

85
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:44,997
The only question is where
does Arterton keep the ledger?

86
00:05:45,038 --> 00:05:46,414
[PATERSON]
I'd wager the classified

87
00:05:46,456 --> 00:05:48,792
materials room on
the fifth floor.

88
00:05:49,709 --> 00:05:51,920
[PATERSON] The chief
inspector is in charge of it.

89
00:05:51,962 --> 00:05:54,422
No one enters
without his permission.

90
00:05:54,464 --> 00:05:58,260
Come on, side-stepping
Arterton? It's impossible!

91
00:05:58,302 --> 00:06:00,512
[PATERSON]
Hmph. And at times like this,

92
00:06:00,553 --> 00:06:03,098
I believe you call on him.

93
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:07,560
Your friend, the great
consulting detective.

94
00:06:07,602 --> 00:06:09,938
You're referring to Holmes?

95
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:11,940
[LESTRADE clears throat]

96
00:06:11,982 --> 00:06:13,859
[LESTRADE]
I highly doubt he'll show any

97
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:16,653
interest in a treasure hunt.

98
00:06:30,625 --> 00:06:34,546
[HERDER] Welcome. I've been
expecting your arrival,

99
00:06:34,587 --> 00:06:36,715
Mister Bonde.

100
00:06:38,133 --> 00:06:39,134
[HERDER]
I took the liberty of having

101
00:06:39,176 --> 00:06:42,221
everything necessary
prepared in this case.

102
00:06:42,262 --> 00:06:45,349
As you know, this will
be an infiltration mission.

103
00:06:45,390 --> 00:06:46,850
Thus, silence is key.

104
00:06:46,892 --> 00:06:48,977
That's why instead
of relying on powder,

105
00:06:49,019 --> 00:06:51,938
this gun fires
using air pressure.

106
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:52,898
The bullets you see there

107
00:06:52,939 --> 00:06:55,108
have been loaded
with a sleeping agent.

108
00:06:55,150 --> 00:06:58,070
So, if you shoot into a wall,
the gas will be released,

109
00:06:58,111 --> 00:07:02,366
rendering anybody unlucky
enough to be nearby unconscious.

110
00:07:02,407 --> 00:07:04,576
[BONDE]
Very nice, you've come through.

111
00:07:04,617 --> 00:07:06,245
Tell me, Herder.

112
00:07:06,286 --> 00:07:07,996
What might that be over there?

113
00:07:08,038 --> 00:07:10,456
Hah! Caught your
attention, I see.

114
00:07:10,498 --> 00:07:13,293
You have a keen eye,
Mister Bonde.

115
00:07:13,335 --> 00:07:17,964
Presenting my
latest engineering feat.

116
00:07:18,006 --> 00:07:19,841
[grunts]

117
00:07:20,550 --> 00:07:23,469
A specially engineered autocar!

118
00:07:27,766 --> 00:07:28,600
[CITIZENS shout]

119
00:07:28,641 --> 00:07:30,769
[ARTERTON grunts, growls]

120
00:07:30,810 --> 00:07:32,437
[knocking]

121
00:07:32,478 --> 00:07:33,479
[AMES]
Excuse me, sir.

122
00:07:33,521 --> 00:07:35,190
- -[door opens]
- -[ARTERTON] Hm?

123
00:07:37,025 --> 00:07:39,152
[ARTERTON]
Yes, Ames, what do you want?

124
00:07:39,194 --> 00:07:40,486
The suspect's lawyer is here

125
00:07:40,528 --> 00:07:43,073
and is demanding to
speak with his client.

126
00:07:43,115 --> 00:07:45,242
We can't have him
getting any ridiculous ideas

127
00:07:45,284 --> 00:07:47,744
before we obtain a confession.

128
00:07:47,786 --> 00:07:50,205
Tell the lawyer that there's
an emergency or something.

129
00:07:50,247 --> 00:07:52,124
That'll send him away.

130
00:07:52,165 --> 00:07:55,001
I'll have this whole
matter sewn up by morning!

131
00:07:55,043 --> 00:07:56,753
[AMES]
Yes, sir.

132
00:07:58,255 --> 00:08:00,173
[PATERSON]
He's on edge.

133
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:03,176
[PATERSON]
It's that late?

134
00:08:03,218 --> 00:08:05,845
Just about time for
my meeting with him.

135
00:08:13,103 --> 00:08:16,231
[PATERSON] Appears this man
is not the punctual type.

136
00:08:16,273 --> 00:08:17,857
[car rumbles]

137
00:08:17,899 --> 00:08:19,443
[PATERSON]
Hm?

138
00:08:31,830 --> 00:08:34,666
[CITIZENS gasp, murmur]

139
00:08:50,556 --> 00:08:53,518
[BONDE]
Sorry for running late.

140
00:08:53,559 --> 00:08:54,478
[BARTENDER 17A]
Evening, sir.

141
00:08:54,519 --> 00:08:55,687
What can I get for you?

142
00:08:55,728 --> 00:08:58,731
Five parts brandy to
one-part maraschino, stirred.

143
00:08:58,773 --> 00:09:01,985
With Angostura bitters,
three dashes, if you would.

144
00:09:02,027 --> 00:09:03,487
Three? Very good.

145
00:09:03,528 --> 00:09:06,281
I'll have that ready
in just a moment.

146
00:09:06,323 --> 00:09:08,825
You're drawing an awful
lot of attention.

147
00:09:08,867 --> 00:09:10,452
[BONDE] It took some
time for its creator

148
00:09:10,494 --> 00:09:13,246
to explain the gears and get
his permission to borrow it.

149
00:09:13,288 --> 00:09:15,790
But not as long as it would
have taken me to arrive here

150
00:09:15,832 --> 00:09:17,792
if I had ridden by
carriage instead.

151
00:09:17,834 --> 00:09:20,420
[PATERSON]
Hm. Point taken.

152
00:09:20,462 --> 00:09:22,506
Headquarters is on
high alert at the moment

153
00:09:22,547 --> 00:09:25,217
thanks to Arterton's orders.

154
00:09:25,258 --> 00:09:28,345
Just to get in it'll
require a double check.

155
00:09:28,387 --> 00:09:32,349
Meaning I can't sneak
you inside on my own.

156
00:09:32,391 --> 00:09:35,268
[MAN 17A]
Oh, come on now, luv.

157
00:09:35,310 --> 00:09:37,937
Yeah, you.

158
00:09:37,979 --> 00:09:40,190
That local doctor
the Yard arrested.

159
00:09:40,232 --> 00:09:43,527
He's already endured hours
of harsh interrogation.

160
00:09:43,568 --> 00:09:45,320
We need to resolve
this affair soon,

161
00:09:45,362 --> 00:09:48,573
before they elicit
a false confession from him.

162
00:09:48,614 --> 00:09:51,284
[BONDE] The secret ledger--
where is it hidden?

163
00:09:51,326 --> 00:09:54,329
We know it's located in the
classified materials room,

164
00:09:54,371 --> 00:09:57,457
but the only way in
is with Arterton's permission.

165
00:09:57,499 --> 00:10:00,960
He has his own hand-picked
officers guarding it.

166
00:10:02,879 --> 00:10:06,174
Your as-yet-unnamed
cocktail, sir. Enjoy.

167
00:10:06,216 --> 00:10:07,675
Many thanks.

168
00:10:07,717 --> 00:10:12,764
That's no obstacle; there isn't
anything I'm not able to steal.

169
00:10:12,805 --> 00:10:15,641
You're very confident.

170
00:10:15,683 --> 00:10:17,018
Would you care for one?

171
00:10:17,060 --> 00:10:19,187
It takes an already
acerbic beverage

172
00:10:19,229 --> 00:10:22,023
and makes it harsher so
you savour the bitters.

173
00:10:22,065 --> 00:10:24,109
Thank you, but no.

174
00:10:26,652 --> 00:10:28,280
[BONDE grunts]
It's quite good.

175
00:10:28,321 --> 00:10:30,740
Think I'll call
this a "Paterson."

176
00:10:30,782 --> 00:10:32,409
Returning to the topic at hand,

177
00:10:32,451 --> 00:10:34,911
you have to find some way
to infiltrate headquarters.

178
00:10:34,953 --> 00:10:37,456
Say, what about disguising
yourself as Lestrade?

179
00:10:37,497 --> 00:10:38,331
- -[glass clatters]

180
00:10:38,373 --> 00:10:39,291
- -Better yet...
- -[glass clatters]

181
00:10:39,332 --> 00:10:41,418
I don't want to
have a drink with you!

182
00:10:41,460 --> 00:10:43,169
[BARTENDER 17A]
Sir, please stop this at once.

183
00:10:43,211 --> 00:10:45,755
[MAN 17A] What's the harm?
Just one little drink.

184
00:10:45,797 --> 00:10:47,715
I prefer a more direct approach.

185
00:10:47,757 --> 00:10:49,842
[WOMAN 17A]
Keep your hands off me!

186
00:10:50,676 --> 00:10:52,887
[MAN 17A groans]

187
00:10:52,929 --> 00:10:56,015
You're clearly bothering
the lady, drunken buffoon.

188
00:10:56,057 --> 00:10:58,477
[MAN 17A]
Hey! What'd you call me?

189
00:10:58,518 --> 00:10:59,227
[WOMAN 17A]
Thank you!

190
00:10:59,269 --> 00:11:00,186
Not at all; it's my pleasure.

191
00:11:00,228 --> 00:11:02,939
- -[glass shatters]
- -[WOMAN 17A gasps]

192
00:11:04,149 --> 00:11:08,820
You tryin' to steal
my date? I'll kill you!

193
00:11:08,861 --> 00:11:10,447
[BONDE chuckles]

194
00:11:12,282 --> 00:11:14,200
[clang]

195
00:11:14,242 --> 00:11:15,868
[PATERSON] Because of
the heightened security,

196
00:11:15,910 --> 00:11:18,788
they can't waste much time
dealing with drunkards.

197
00:11:18,830 --> 00:11:19,998
Consider yourself lucky

198
00:11:20,039 --> 00:11:22,959
the officers didn't bother
to frisk you thoroughly.

199
00:11:24,043 --> 00:11:27,839
[BONDE] I got inside
the building, didn't I?

200
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:31,384
[sighs] I brought
some good news.

201
00:11:31,426 --> 00:11:33,386
Lestrade just returned
from his errand.

202
00:11:33,428 --> 00:11:35,597
He's asking for me.

203
00:11:35,639 --> 00:11:36,973
He has a guest with him--

204
00:11:37,015 --> 00:11:38,391
someone whom he couldn't
get admitted

205
00:11:38,433 --> 00:11:41,520
thanks to the double checks
we've implemented.

206
00:11:41,561 --> 00:11:43,938
I think I know
where this is going.

207
00:11:43,980 --> 00:11:46,566
[PATERSON]
Yes, just as you hoped.

208
00:11:46,608 --> 00:11:50,028
The great consulting
detective has arrived.

209
00:11:50,069 --> 00:11:52,531
Meaning all of
the players are here.

210
00:11:52,572 --> 00:11:55,158
Time to begin the operation.

211
00:11:59,912 --> 00:12:06,378
[ARTERTON panting]

212
00:12:06,419 --> 00:12:09,881
Doesn't seem to be protesting
his innocence anymore.

213
00:12:09,922 --> 00:12:11,924
Take over from here.

214
00:12:11,966 --> 00:12:14,010
[AMES]
Very well, sir.

215
00:12:35,990 --> 00:12:37,492
[CITIZENS]
Send out the Ripper!

216
00:12:37,534 --> 00:12:41,746
Send out the Ripper!
Send out the Ripper!

217
00:12:41,787 --> 00:12:43,081
[PATERSON]
I'm impressed you managed

218
00:12:43,122 --> 00:12:45,250
to convince Holmes to show up.

219
00:12:45,291 --> 00:12:47,126
Truth is, it should've
been harder,

220
00:12:47,168 --> 00:12:51,047
but before I could even explain
the situation to him, he said:

221
00:12:51,089 --> 00:12:53,633
I guarantee the man
in custody was framed.

222
00:12:53,675 --> 00:12:55,260
I'll assist you.

223
00:12:55,301 --> 00:12:56,720
His words.

224
00:12:56,761 --> 00:12:59,055
You're quite the
negotiator, Lestrade.

225
00:12:59,097 --> 00:13:02,267
Sherlock, I know you
want to exonerate this man,

226
00:13:02,308 --> 00:13:04,728
but an expedition
through Scotland Yard?

227
00:13:04,769 --> 00:13:06,688
For a ledger that
may not even exist?

228
00:13:06,730 --> 00:13:08,064
Look, John,

229
00:13:08,106 --> 00:13:11,317
I told you the true story about
Jack the Ripper, didn't I?

230
00:13:11,359 --> 00:13:14,320
I still need to
verify something.

231
00:13:14,362 --> 00:13:15,905
Understood.

232
00:13:15,947 --> 00:13:17,616
[HOLMES]
I know it was the Lord of Crime

233
00:13:17,657 --> 00:13:19,451
who solved the Ripper slayings,

234
00:13:19,492 --> 00:13:22,078
and if he really does
fancy himself Robin Hood,

235
00:13:22,120 --> 00:13:23,329
he'll most likely step in

236
00:13:23,371 --> 00:13:26,416
concerning this
unjustified arrest.

237
00:13:26,458 --> 00:13:27,709
Problem is,

238
00:13:27,751 --> 00:13:29,628
if I'm not careful
how I go about this,

239
00:13:29,669 --> 00:13:32,004
I'll upset the chain
of events he had planned.

240
00:13:32,046 --> 00:13:33,214
Worst case scenario,

241
00:13:33,256 --> 00:13:35,841
that means being unable
to rescue an innocent man.

242
00:13:35,883 --> 00:13:38,136
- -[HOLMES groans]
- -[WATSON gasps]

243
00:13:38,177 --> 00:13:40,054
[HOLMES]
That miserable sod!

244
00:13:40,096 --> 00:13:42,682
He infuriates me every time!

245
00:13:42,724 --> 00:13:45,518
Of course, he accounted
for me being here tonight.

246
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:46,811
At no point have I had

247
00:13:46,852 --> 00:13:50,189
the freedom to make
a single decision.

248
00:13:50,231 --> 00:13:51,733
The way things are going,

249
00:13:51,775 --> 00:13:54,527
I'll be trapped in the
palm of his hand forever!

250
00:13:54,569 --> 00:13:55,987
[WATSON]
Sherlock.

251
00:13:56,028 --> 00:13:56,780
Sherlock!

252
00:13:56,821 --> 00:13:58,698
[HOLMES gasps]

253
00:13:58,740 --> 00:14:01,326
The inspector was about
to lead us upstairs.

254
00:14:01,367 --> 00:14:03,161
Oh, right-o.

255
00:14:06,872 --> 00:14:08,541
[HOLMES]
Fine then, I'll go along

256
00:14:08,583 --> 00:14:11,795
and make the choices
I believe are right.

257
00:14:11,836 --> 00:14:15,340
It's up to me whether
I play his game or not.

258
00:14:17,049 --> 00:14:17,759
[LESTRADE grunts]

259
00:14:17,801 --> 00:14:19,135
[BOTH groan]

260
00:14:19,177 --> 00:14:21,053
We weren't sleeping
on the job, I swear!

261
00:14:21,095 --> 00:14:24,307
Access to this wing's
restricted, sir.

262
00:14:24,349 --> 00:14:28,186
I have business in the
classified materials room.

263
00:14:28,227 --> 00:14:30,438
And as you can see,
Chief Inspector Arterton

264
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:33,024
supplied me with
the key to get inside.

265
00:14:33,065 --> 00:14:34,484
[LESTRADE]
Which of course is a blatant lie

266
00:14:34,526 --> 00:14:37,236
only Holmes could
convince me would work.

267
00:14:37,278 --> 00:14:38,404
After you.

268
00:14:38,446 --> 00:14:39,905
[HOLMES]
Right.

269
00:14:41,032 --> 00:14:42,241
Beg your pardon, Inspector,

270
00:14:42,283 --> 00:14:45,411
but no one informed us you
would be demanding access.

271
00:14:45,453 --> 00:14:46,830
[LESTRADE]
It concerns something that

272
00:14:46,871 --> 00:14:48,456
came up rather suddenly.

273
00:14:48,498 --> 00:14:50,709
[GUARD 17A]
So, these two are--?

274
00:14:50,750 --> 00:14:52,168
Let's go.

275
00:14:52,210 --> 00:14:53,294
Inspector!

276
00:14:53,336 --> 00:14:55,547
These two are helping
with the investigation.

277
00:14:55,588 --> 00:14:58,800
They also have Chief Inspector
Arterton's authorization.

278
00:14:58,842 --> 00:15:01,511
The chief inspector
gave his key to Lestrade?

279
00:15:01,553 --> 00:15:02,970
Does that sound right?

280
00:15:03,012 --> 00:15:04,597
No.

281
00:15:04,639 --> 00:15:06,474
[LESTRADE]
There's the room!

282
00:15:06,516 --> 00:15:08,226
The rest is up to you, Holmes.

283
00:15:08,267 --> 00:15:10,019
[HOLMES]
Sure. Leave it to me.

284
00:15:10,061 --> 00:15:11,145
[lock clinks]

285
00:15:11,187 --> 00:15:12,355
[BONDE gasps]

286
00:15:12,397 --> 00:15:14,065
[BONDE]
Sherlock!

287
00:15:14,106 --> 00:15:16,401
[BONDE chuckles]

288
00:15:16,442 --> 00:15:17,943
[BONDE blows]

289
00:15:19,362 --> 00:15:20,780
[ARTERTON]
Hold it!

290
00:15:22,323 --> 00:15:24,242
That was fast.

291
00:15:26,411 --> 00:15:29,121
I can't wait to hear
your explanation, Lestrade.

292
00:15:29,163 --> 00:15:30,832
During a crisis
while we're in lockdown,

293
00:15:30,874 --> 00:15:32,792
not only do you
enter a restricted area

294
00:15:32,834 --> 00:15:35,837
without my permission, but you
bring civilians with you!

295
00:15:35,879 --> 00:15:39,257
Answer me! Just what
are you plotting?

296
00:15:39,298 --> 00:15:42,093
[gasps] And we're going
to let him go unchallenged

297
00:15:42,134 --> 00:15:43,969
while he convicts
an innocent man?

298
00:15:44,011 --> 00:15:47,265
Scotland Yard is
supposed to uphold the law!

299
00:15:47,306 --> 00:15:50,560
The case against the doctor
needs to be re-examined.

300
00:15:50,602 --> 00:15:52,562
Wh-What was that?

301
00:15:54,230 --> 00:15:55,565
[LESTRADE]
You heard me.

302
00:15:55,607 --> 00:15:58,735
I believe the man in our custody
has been wrongly accused!

303
00:15:58,777 --> 00:16:02,530
[ARTERTON grunts] Lestrade,
you would dare to defy me?

304
00:16:02,572 --> 00:16:04,031
[LESTRADE]
On the other side of that door,

305
00:16:04,073 --> 00:16:05,991
there's proof that'll
back up my claim.

306
00:16:06,033 --> 00:16:08,327
A ledger that shows you
falsified evidence!

307
00:16:08,369 --> 00:16:10,413
[gasps, grunts]

308
00:16:10,455 --> 00:16:12,206
You're the one peddling lies!

309
00:16:12,248 --> 00:16:13,249
- -[ARTERTON grunts]
- -[LESTRADE groans]

310
00:16:13,291 --> 00:16:16,043
If I say a man's guilty,
then he is guilty!

311
00:16:16,085 --> 00:16:19,338
And I will never give you
my permission to go in there!

312
00:16:19,380 --> 00:16:22,049
If you try breaking into this
room just one more time,

313
00:16:22,091 --> 00:16:24,552
I'll have you
immediately arrested!

314
00:16:24,594 --> 00:16:26,095
[LESTRADE]
Based on his reaction,

315
00:16:26,137 --> 00:16:31,350
no question remains--
the ledger's behind that door.

316
00:16:31,392 --> 00:16:33,645
[grunts]

317
00:16:33,686 --> 00:16:37,440
- -[thudding]
- -[ARTERTON, LESTRADE grunting]

318
00:16:39,734 --> 00:16:40,526
- -[WATSON gasps]
- -[OFFICERS shouting]

319
00:16:40,568 --> 00:16:41,778
[OFFICER 17A]
Inspector, please!

320
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:43,028
Enough!

321
00:16:43,070 --> 00:16:45,740
I won't let you convict an
innocent man, you hear me?

322
00:16:45,782 --> 00:16:47,784
[LESTRADE grunts]

323
00:16:47,826 --> 00:16:49,577
Lestrade's got
some fight in him.

324
00:16:49,619 --> 00:16:51,704
Right.

325
00:16:51,746 --> 00:16:53,122
[LESTRADE]
I said let me go!

326
00:16:53,164 --> 00:16:55,959
[ARTERTON groans]
Someone, go and get help!

327
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,253
Toss Lestrade into
a cell, you hear me?!

328
00:16:59,295 --> 00:17:01,297
Yes, sir.

329
00:17:05,384 --> 00:17:10,181
[ARTERTON, OFFICERS,
LESTRADE yelling]

330
00:17:12,224 --> 00:17:13,935
Well, John, c'mon.

331
00:17:13,977 --> 00:17:16,688
What? But Inspector Lestrade...

332
00:17:16,729 --> 00:17:18,105
[HOLMES]
Never mind.

333
00:17:36,290 --> 00:17:37,500
My word,

334
00:17:37,542 --> 00:17:40,670
how many more friends will
I have to visit down here?

335
00:17:41,838 --> 00:17:43,006
[LESTRADE]
Paterson?

336
00:17:43,047 --> 00:17:45,341
That was far too rash,
even for you.

337
00:17:45,383 --> 00:17:47,010
I feel so ashamed.

338
00:17:47,051 --> 00:17:49,804
[PATERSON chuckles]
Rash, but not surprising.

339
00:17:49,846 --> 00:17:51,556
Brought you something.

340
00:17:51,597 --> 00:17:55,059
[gasps] You mean-- That isn't...

341
00:17:55,100 --> 00:17:56,352
It can't be!

342
00:17:56,394 --> 00:17:58,437
Arterton's secret ledger.

343
00:17:58,479 --> 00:18:00,439
Whilst you were having
your donnybrook in

344
00:18:00,481 --> 00:18:02,441
the classified materials room.

345
00:18:02,483 --> 00:18:04,777
I dipped into his
office and lifted it.

346
00:18:04,819 --> 00:18:07,780
Despite saying it wasn't
there to begin with.

347
00:18:07,822 --> 00:18:11,367
[laughing]

348
00:18:11,409 --> 00:18:13,036
Yes, well, in retrospect,

349
00:18:13,077 --> 00:18:15,914
I ended up treating you
and the others as decoys.

350
00:18:15,955 --> 00:18:17,164
I apologize.

351
00:18:17,206 --> 00:18:18,457
Happy to oblige.

352
00:18:18,499 --> 00:18:20,334
As long as it helps
save an innocent man,

353
00:18:20,376 --> 00:18:22,754
you'll get no
complaints from me.

354
00:18:25,297 --> 00:18:26,925
[PATERSON] All that
remains is for you to hand

355
00:18:26,966 --> 00:18:28,927
the ledger over to Holmes.

356
00:18:30,344 --> 00:18:32,805
Seems Arterton's time
as head of the CID

357
00:18:32,847 --> 00:18:34,849
is rapidly drawing to a close.

358
00:18:34,891 --> 00:18:38,269
And I for one can't wait!
Leave it to me, Paterson!

359
00:18:42,023 --> 00:18:44,984
[PATERSON] It appears
everything is going to work,

360
00:18:45,026 --> 00:18:47,194
but I have to say...

361
00:18:48,613 --> 00:18:49,822
[PATERSON]
Hell's bells.

362
00:18:49,864 --> 00:18:52,366
That chap is bloody mad.

363
00:18:54,285 --> 00:18:55,494
[PATERSON]
Still, though,

364
00:18:55,536 --> 00:18:58,748
our mutual friend admitted
him into the organization.

365
00:18:58,790 --> 00:19:02,501
So, he must be good.

366
00:19:02,543 --> 00:19:04,295
[PATERSON chuckles]

367
00:19:23,397 --> 00:19:26,067
Is that the article
about the Ripper case?

368
00:19:26,109 --> 00:19:30,195
It is. Looks like the disgraced
chief inspector's out of a job.

369
00:19:30,237 --> 00:19:34,533
As for the rest of his cronies,
seems they got the axe, as well.

370
00:19:34,575 --> 00:19:36,619
[WATSON grunts]
Perhaps most importantly,

371
00:19:36,661 --> 00:19:38,496
a citizen having
nothing to do with the case

372
00:19:38,537 --> 00:19:40,164
is no longer behind bars.

373
00:19:40,205 --> 00:19:43,375
And Lestrade's show of courage
was a surprise, as well.

374
00:19:43,417 --> 00:19:45,128
[HOLMES]
I suppose.

375
00:19:47,130 --> 00:19:49,799
[HOLMES] This whole
"Jack the Ripper "incident

376
00:19:49,841 --> 00:19:51,926
and the false accusation--

377
00:19:51,968 --> 00:19:54,720
he secretly insinuated
himself into both cases,

378
00:19:54,762 --> 00:19:57,849
and solved them more
favourably than I would have.

379
00:19:57,890 --> 00:20:01,102
Only one truth can
be derived from this.

380
00:20:01,144 --> 00:20:05,439
He is a Robin Hood--
there's no mistaking that.

381
00:20:05,481 --> 00:20:09,401
But at the same time,
he's also a criminal.

382
00:20:09,443 --> 00:20:11,070
Makes me wonder.

383
00:20:11,112 --> 00:20:13,781
True, he rescued
an innocent man,

384
00:20:13,823 --> 00:20:17,451
but was setting him free
his only objective?

385
00:20:19,078 --> 00:20:20,204
[BARTENDER 17A]
Evening, sir.

386
00:20:20,245 --> 00:20:21,622
What can I get for you?

387
00:20:21,664 --> 00:20:23,541
[PATERSON]
Well, let's see.

388
00:20:23,582 --> 00:20:25,710
How about a brandy
and maraschino?

389
00:20:25,751 --> 00:20:29,088
With Angostura bitters,
three dashes, please.

390
00:20:29,130 --> 00:20:31,090
Excellent choice of drink, sir.

391
00:20:31,132 --> 00:20:34,135
Sounds like one unusual
cocktail you've ordered there.

392
00:20:34,177 --> 00:20:35,386
[PATERSON chuckles]

393
00:20:35,427 --> 00:20:37,471
A friend of mine's rather
partial to drinking them.

394
00:20:37,513 --> 00:20:40,016
And they've gotten me curious
as to how they taste now.

395
00:20:40,058 --> 00:20:41,767
I see.

396
00:20:41,809 --> 00:20:45,354
You know, I'm sorry things
turned out the way they did.

397
00:20:45,396 --> 00:20:48,107
[PATERSON] Everyone wanted
you to throw that punch.

398
00:20:48,149 --> 00:20:51,569
"The hero who managed to lance
that collective boil of ours."

399
00:20:51,610 --> 00:20:55,031
When you put it that way, sounds
like you're the real hero.

400
00:20:55,073 --> 00:20:57,075
If it hadn't been
for you taking action,

401
00:20:57,116 --> 00:20:59,618
things would have ended up
a lot differently.

402
00:20:59,660 --> 00:21:00,619
[PATERSON]
Hm.

403
00:21:00,661 --> 00:21:02,538
Everything worked out fine.

404
00:21:02,580 --> 00:21:04,248
Besides I've been
wanting to get out

405
00:21:04,289 --> 00:21:07,168
and pursue cases in
the field a little more.

406
00:21:07,210 --> 00:21:08,419
[PATERSON chuckles]

407
00:21:08,460 --> 00:21:12,131
Knowing you, that doesn't
surprise me in the least.

408
00:21:12,173 --> 00:21:14,717
Thank you both for waiting;
here's two Patersons.

409
00:21:14,759 --> 00:21:15,676
Huh?

410
00:21:15,718 --> 00:21:17,302
[PATERSON chuckles]

411
00:21:17,344 --> 00:21:19,555
[LESTRADE] My apologies for
leaving you all the hassle

412
00:21:19,597 --> 00:21:22,850
and paperwork that comes
with the chief inspector's post.

413
00:21:22,892 --> 00:21:24,351
[PATERSON]
Don't mention it.

414
00:21:24,393 --> 00:21:26,645
We're chums, aren't we?

415
00:21:26,687 --> 00:21:29,398
[LESTRADE] Cheers,
Paterson, to your promotion.

416
00:21:29,440 --> 00:21:30,775
[glass clinks]

417
00:21:32,693 --> 00:21:35,446
[LESTRADE]
Ugh, too bitter!

418
00:21:35,487 --> 00:21:37,782
[WILLIAM] The corruption
within the Yard has been purged,

419
00:21:37,823 --> 00:21:40,826
and the top CID post
has been reassigned.

420
00:21:40,868 --> 00:21:44,622
Coincidentally, our inside
man has moved up in rank.

421
00:21:44,663 --> 00:21:48,584
Thanks to his new title,
he'll be of great use to us.

422
00:21:49,585 --> 00:21:52,880
Another step closer
to the Moriarty Plan.

423
00:21:52,922 --> 00:21:54,882
We're almost there.

424
00:23:32,646 --> 00:23:35,399
[BONDE] Next time
on Moriarty the Patriot  :

425
00:23:35,440 --> 00:23:37,985
"The Merchant of London."
